# CODEOWNERS — example
#
# Copy to .github/CODEOWNERS in each repo and adapt the paths.
# Replace @goldseats-org/... teams and @founder with real handles before committing.
#
# How GitHub uses this:
#  - The LAST matching pattern wins. Order matters, so general rules go first and
#    specific overrides go last.
#  - Listed owners are auto-requested as reviewers on any PR touching their paths.
#  - Combined with "Require review from Code Owners" in the branch protection ruleset,
#    an owner's approval becomes mandatory for those paths.
#
# Caveat worth knowing up front: with a one-person team, CODEOWNERS auto-requests
# review from yourself, which GitHub silently ignores. That's fine — the file's value
# right now is that it documents intended ownership, and it starts working the day a
# second person joins. See engineering/code-review.md for the solo-team process.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default — everything has an owner
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                                   @founder


# ===========================================================================
# goldseats-api
# ===========================================================================

# The scoring engine is the product. Changes here need the most careful review:
# a weight change without a before/after distribution gets request-changes.
/app/ml/                            @founder
/app/ml/scoring.py                  @founder
/tests/fixtures/golden/             @founder
/tests/unit/ml/                     @founder

# Schema changes are permanent in a way most code isn't. Every migration needs a
# tested downgrade, expand/contract compliance, and SQLite/Postgres parity.
/alembic/                           @founder
/app/db/models/                     @founder

# The public API contract. goldseats-web generates its types from this, so a
# careless response shape becomes permanent the moment the web app depends on it.
/app/api/                           @founder
/app/schemas/                       @founder

# Auth. Small mistakes here are large mistakes.
/app/api/v1/auth.py                 @founder
/app/domain/auth.py                 @founder

# Chatbot guardrails. The grounding verifier is the most security-relevant code
# in the repo — it's what stops an invented seat reaching a user.
/app/chat/                          @founder
/app/chat/guardrails.py             @founder
/tests/chat/                        @founder

# Theatre seed data. Hand-authored, and wrong data here means wrong
# recommendations for real people at real cinemas.
/seeds/                             @founder
/app/cli/seed_theatre.py            @founder

# Ingestion — every source has legal obligations attached.
/app/ingest/                        @founder

# Configuration surface.
/app/config.py                      @founder
/.env.example                       @founder


# ===========================================================================
# goldseats-web
# ===========================================================================

# Design tokens. #FFD700 lives here and nowhere else.
/tailwind.config.ts                 @founder
/src/app/layout.tsx                 @founder

# The seat map is the most accessibility-sensitive component in the product:
# grid semantics, roving focus, and no information by colour alone.
/src/components/seats/              @founder

# 3D view. Performance budgets and honest geometry both matter here.
/src/components/seats-3d/           @founder
/src/lib/three/                     @founder

# Shared UI kit. Changes ripple everywhere.
/src/components/ui/                 @founder

# API client and generated types.
/src/lib/api/                       @founder
/src/types/api.ts                   @founder

/src/lib/env.ts                     @founder
/.env.example                       @founder


# ===========================================================================
# Shared across all repos
# ===========================================================================

# CI and anything that can deploy.
/.github/                           @founder
/.github/workflows/                 @founder

# Dependency manifests — supply chain.
/package.json                       @founder
/package-lock.json                  @founder
/requirements.txt                   @founder
/requirements-dev.txt               @founder
/pyproject.toml                     @founder


# ===========================================================================
# goldseats-docs
# ===========================================================================

# ADRs are immutable once accepted. A change here is either a typo fix or a
# superseding decision, and those are different conversations.
/architecture/adr/                  @founder

# Legal documents. Both need a lawyer's review before launch, and the
# data-sources policy is binding on every contributor.
/legal/                             @founder

# Conventions everyone follows.
/engineering/                       @founder

# Runbooks are read under pressure. Wrong ones are worse than missing ones.
/operations/runbooks/               @founder


# ===========================================================================
# When a second engineer joins
# ===========================================================================
#
# Replace individual handles with teams so ownership survives someone being on
# holiday:
#
#   @goldseats-org/backend     — api, database, scoring
#   @goldseats-org/frontend    — web, UI, 3D
#   @goldseats-org/maintainers — CI, docs, legal, ADRs
#
# Example:
#
#   *                       @goldseats-org/maintainers
#   /app/                   @goldseats-org/backend
#   /src/                   @goldseats-org/frontend
#   /app/ml/                @goldseats-org/backend @goldseats-org/maintainers
#   /legal/                 @goldseats-org/maintainers
#   /architecture/adr/      @goldseats-org/maintainers
#
# Two owners on a path means both must approve. Reserve that for genuinely
# irreversible things — the scoring engine, migrations, and legal documents —
# rather than applying it broadly, or every PR needs two reviewers and nothing
# ships.
#
# At that point also update engineering/code-review.md, which currently documents
# a degraded self-review process for a one-person team.
